1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,804
Sparse attention benchmarking and optimization.

2
00:00:02,804 --> 00:00:06,444
This video walks through the whole project in plain language:

3
00:00:06,444 --> 00:00:11,217
the problem, the idea, our code, how we measured it, and what every graph means.

4
00:00:11,583 --> 00:00:12,259
Four parts.

5
00:00:12,259 --> 00:00:15,389
First, what attention is and why it gets expensive.

6
00:00:15,389 --> 00:00:18,766
Second, our block sparse kernel and how we improved it.

7
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:21,896
Third, the benchmarking framework and every result.

8
00:00:21,896 --> 00:00:26,132
And last, why the results look the way they do, and what we conclude.

9
00:00:27,083 --> 00:00:28,117
Take the sentence:

10
00:00:28,117 --> 00:00:30,013
the cat sat because it was tired.

11
00:00:30,013 --> 00:00:34,723
To understand the word it, a language model has to work out that it means the cat.

12
00:00:34,723 --> 00:00:35,872
Attention does this:

13
00:00:35,872 --> 00:00:40,237
every word looks at every other word, and decides how much each one matters.

14
00:00:40,625 --> 00:00:44,500
Each word is turned into three lists of sixty four numbers.

15
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:45,025
A query:

16
00:00:45,025 --> 00:00:46,470
what am I looking for.

17
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:46,864
A key:

18
00:00:46,864 --> 00:00:47,914
what do I offer.

19
00:00:47,914 --> 00:00:48,702
And a value:

20
00:00:48,702 --> 00:00:50,410
the information I pass on.

21
00:00:50,792 --> 00:00:51,810
Then three steps.

22
00:00:51,810 --> 00:00:52,050
One:

23
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:56,904
score every pair of words, by comparing one word's query with another word's key.

24
00:00:56,904 --> 00:00:59,600
That makes a table with N rows and N columns.

25
00:00:59,600 --> 00:00:59,840
Two:

26
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:03,735
softmax turns each row of scores into weights that add up to one.

27
00:01:03,735 --> 00:01:04,094
Three:

28
00:01:04,094 --> 00:01:07,510
each word's output is the weighted average of the values.

29
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:10,446
So it ends up mostly carrying the meaning of cat.

30
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:11,629
As a formula:

31
00:01:11,629 --> 00:01:16,157
softmax of Q times K transpose, over the square root of the size, times V.

32
00:01:16,157 --> 00:01:20,501
Code that computes this on real hardware is called an attention kernel.

33
00:01:21,458 --> 00:01:23,022
The problem is that table.

34
00:01:23,022 --> 00:01:26,750
It has N times N entries, so when the text gets twice as long,

35
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:28,735
the table gets four times bigger:

36
00:01:28,735 --> 00:01:31,501
four times the work and four times the memory.

37
00:01:31,875 --> 00:01:34,970
At sixteen thousand tokens with eight attention heads,

38
00:01:34,970 --> 00:01:40,357
one pass needs five hundred and fifty billion operations, and the table takes eight gigabytes.

39
00:01:40,357 --> 00:01:42,936
That's half the memory of the laptop we used.

40
00:01:43,917 --> 00:01:45,019
The idea is simple.

41
00:01:45,019 --> 00:01:48,730
Most words only need their neighbours and a few important words.

42
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:52,441
So we cut the big table into blocks of sixty four by sixty four,

43
00:01:52,441 --> 00:01:54,761
and only compute the blocks that matter.

44
00:01:54,761 --> 00:01:56,907
A skipped block costs nothing at all.

45
00:01:57,292 --> 00:01:59,833
Which blocks to keep is called the pattern.

46
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:01,193
Our framework has five.

47
00:02:01,193 --> 00:02:02,552
Dense keeps everything.

48
00:02:02,552 --> 00:02:04,503
Local keeps a band of neighbours.

49
00:02:04,503 --> 00:02:06,689
Strided adds regular summary columns.

50
00:02:06,689 --> 00:02:09,999
Longformer adds a few global blocks that see everything.

51
00:02:09,999 --> 00:02:13,664
And random keeps the same number of random blocks in each row.

52
00:02:14,042 --> 00:02:16,666
The fraction of blocks we keep is the density.

53
00:02:16,666 --> 00:02:20,660
Keep one block in eight, and the density is twelve and a half percent,

54
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:23,113
so ideally the work is eight times smaller.

55
00:02:23,113 --> 00:02:23,855
Our question:

56
00:02:23,855 --> 00:02:26,651
how close does a real computer get to that ideal?

57
00:02:27,042 --> 00:02:28,157
One important point.

58
00:02:28,157 --> 00:02:31,726
A lazy way to do sparse attention is to compute the whole table,

59
00:02:31,726 --> 00:02:33,900
and then hide the parts you don't want.

60
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:38,696
That gives the right answer, but saves nothing, because all the work was already done.

61
00:02:38,696 --> 00:02:42,265
Real savings only come if the skipped blocks are never computed.

62
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:44,448
Here is our kernel.

63
00:02:44,448 --> 00:02:49,745
For each block of sixty four queries, we look up which key blocks the pattern keeps.

64
00:02:49,745 --> 00:02:55,043
We fetch only those keys and values, and run ordinary attention on that small piece:

65
00:02:55,043 --> 00:02:56,934
scores, softmax, weighted sum.

66
00:02:56,934 --> 00:02:59,268
Then we move to the next query block.

67
00:02:59,625 --> 00:03:01,482
In code it really is five lines.

68
00:03:01,482 --> 00:03:02,817
We wrote it in PyTorch.

69
00:03:02,817 --> 00:03:05,835
PyTorch does the arithmetic, like any library would.

70
00:03:05,835 --> 00:03:09,781
Our code decides which blocks are computed and how they are fetched,

71
00:03:09,781 --> 00:03:12,335
and that is where all the savings come from.

72
00:03:12,708 --> 00:03:15,523
Before running, we turn the pattern into a plan:

73
00:03:15,523 --> 00:03:19,509
a short list for each query block, saying which key blocks it needs.

74
00:03:19,509 --> 00:03:24,903
The plan is made once and reused, just like a real model reuses its pattern for every input.

75
00:03:25,292 --> 00:03:27,013
Is the answer still correct?

76
00:03:27,013 --> 00:03:27,259
Yes.

77
00:03:27,259 --> 00:03:30,947
We compare every version against a high precision reference:

78
00:03:30,947 --> 00:03:35,619
one hundred and sixty eight checks, all passing on both the CPU and the GPU,

79
00:03:35,619 --> 00:03:39,430
with errors around one in a million, which is normal rounding.

80
00:03:40,417 --> 00:03:45,205
Our first version copied the needed key and value blocks into a new piece of memory

81
00:03:45,205 --> 00:03:46,302
before multiplying.

82
00:03:46,302 --> 00:03:47,628
But copying takes time.

83
00:03:47,628 --> 00:03:50,629
In version two we noticed that for a sliding window,

84
00:03:50,629 --> 00:03:53,917
the needed blocks sit right next to each other in memory.

85
00:03:53,917 --> 00:03:56,456
So we simply point at them, without copying.

86
00:03:56,456 --> 00:03:57,956
PyTorch calls that a view.

87
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:00,152
We also tried a different idea:

88
00:04:00,152 --> 00:04:03,671
do all query blocks in one giant multiply instead of a loop.

89
00:04:03,671 --> 00:04:08,363
The catch is that every row must have the same length, so short rows get padded.

90
00:04:08,363 --> 00:04:13,818
With Longformer, one global row needs all sixty four blocks while the others need about nine,

91
00:04:13,818 --> 00:04:16,164
so everything gets padded to sixty four.

92
00:04:16,164 --> 00:04:17,747
Most of the work is wasted.

93
00:04:18,125 --> 00:04:20,619
Here are the four versions, measured.

94
00:04:20,619 --> 00:04:21,495
Masked dense:

95
00:04:21,495 --> 00:04:24,124
two hundred and forty two milliseconds.

96
00:04:24,124 --> 00:04:26,280
Version one, which skips blocks:

97
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:27,022
thirty two.

98
00:04:27,022 --> 00:04:29,246
Version two, which avoids copies:

99
00:04:29,246 --> 00:04:34,773
twenty five on the CPU, and fifteen on the GPU, which is very sensitive to copies.

100
00:04:34,773 --> 00:04:37,132
And the padded batch on Longformer:

101
00:04:37,132 --> 00:04:38,412
over three hundred.

102
00:04:38,412 --> 00:04:41,445
So we kept version two, and rejected padding.

103
00:04:42,417 --> 00:04:45,925
To measure everything, we built a benchmarking framework.

104
00:04:45,925 --> 00:04:50,849
One command runs every experiment, and saves a results table and a log for each.

105
00:04:50,849 --> 00:04:53,127
Another command draws all the graphs.

106
00:04:53,127 --> 00:04:57,928
Pattern, density, sequence length, block size and device are all configurable.

107
00:04:58,292 --> 00:05:03,530
Time is the median of seven runs after a warm up, so one slow run doesn't distort it.

108
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:05,503
Throughput is tokens per second.

109
00:05:05,503 --> 00:05:08,523
Computation is counted with the standard formula.

110
00:05:08,523 --> 00:05:12,344
And memory is measured by running one call in a fresh process,

111
00:05:12,344 --> 00:05:16,103
and asking the operating system how much memory it peaked at.

112
00:05:16,103 --> 00:05:19,863
Everything ran on an Apple M4 laptop, on the CPU and the GPU.

113
00:05:20,833 --> 00:05:21,680
First result:

114
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:24,352
time as the text gets longer, on the CPU.

115
00:05:24,352 --> 00:05:29,174
Both axes are logarithmic, so the slope tells you how fast the cost grows.

116
00:05:29,174 --> 00:05:31,129
Dense attention has slope two.

117
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:32,237
That's quadratic:

118
00:05:32,237 --> 00:05:34,778
double the length, four times the time.

119
00:05:35,167 --> 00:05:38,395
Our local window and Longformer kernels have slope one.

120
00:05:38,395 --> 00:05:42,972
That's linear, because each query block always sees the same number of blocks,

121
00:05:42,972 --> 00:05:44,440
however long the text is.

122
00:05:44,792 --> 00:05:49,685
At eight thousand tokens, dense takes seven hundred and sixty four milliseconds.

123
00:05:49,685 --> 00:05:51,704
Our local window takes fifty two.

124
00:05:51,704 --> 00:05:54,212
That's fourteen point seven times faster,

125
00:05:54,212 --> 00:05:57,577
almost exactly the fourteen and a half times less work.

126
00:05:57,577 --> 00:06:01,492
At sixteen thousand tokens, dense can't even run on this laptop.

127
00:06:01,492 --> 00:06:04,245
Ours takes one hundred and four milliseconds,

128
00:06:04,245 --> 00:06:07,915
thirteen times faster than PyTorch's own built in attention.

129
00:06:08,875 --> 00:06:10,797
Throughput tells the same story.

130
00:06:10,797 --> 00:06:16,084
Our local window handles about one hundred and fifty seven thousand tokens per second at

131
00:06:16,084 --> 00:06:19,508
every length, while dense drops to under eleven thousand.

132
00:06:19,508 --> 00:06:21,310
And the amount of computation:

133
00:06:21,310 --> 00:06:26,116
at sixteen thousand tokens, five hundred and fifty billion operations for dense,

134
00:06:26,116 --> 00:06:28,219
but only nineteen billion for ours.

135
00:06:28,219 --> 00:06:29,600
Twenty nine times less.

136
00:06:30,583 --> 00:06:35,759
Next, we fix the length at four thousand tokens and change how many blocks we keep.

137
00:06:35,759 --> 00:06:38,690
The dotted line is the ideal, one over density.

138
00:06:38,690 --> 00:06:40,436
All four patterns follow it.

139
00:06:40,436 --> 00:06:44,240
At twelve and a half percent, we're five to six times faster.

140
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,984
At about three percent, around twelve times.

141
00:06:47,375 --> 00:06:50,322
And the pink line is masked dense, the lazy way.

142
00:06:50,322 --> 00:06:52,593
It never gets faster, at any density.

143
00:06:52,593 --> 00:06:58,242
It's actually a bit slower than plain dense, because hiding the unwanted part is extra work.

144
00:06:58,242 --> 00:07:00,882
This is the clearest result of the project:

145
00:07:00,882 --> 00:07:02,846
skipping helps, masking doesn't.

146
00:07:03,833 --> 00:07:04,236
Memory.

147
00:07:04,236 --> 00:07:07,976
Dense attention keeps two big tables, the scores and the weights.

148
00:07:07,976 --> 00:07:12,866
At eight thousand tokens our formula predicts four thousand and ninety six megabytes,

149
00:07:12,866 --> 00:07:15,513
and we measured four thousand and ninety nine.

150
00:07:15,513 --> 00:07:20,863
Our kernel only ever holds one small piece, so it needs about five megabytes at every length:

151
00:07:20,863 --> 00:07:23,337
roughly seven hundred and sixty times less.

152
00:07:23,337 --> 00:07:27,940
That's what lets sparse attention handle lengths that dense attention can't fit.

153
00:07:28,917 --> 00:07:30,502
Block size is a trade off.

154
00:07:30,502 --> 00:07:33,488
Small blocks of sixteen follow a pattern closely,

155
00:07:33,488 --> 00:07:38,487
so ninety seven percent of the work is useful, but there are many tiny multiplies,

156
00:07:38,487 --> 00:07:39,523
and that is slow.

157
00:07:39,523 --> 00:07:44,400
Big blocks of one hundred and twenty eight waste nineteen percent of their work,

158
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:49,337
but run faster, because computers prefer a few big operations to many small ones.

159
00:07:49,337 --> 00:07:51,410
We chose sixty four, as a balance.

160
00:07:51,792 --> 00:07:52,270
Threads.

161
00:07:52,270 --> 00:07:56,996
PyTorch uses four threads by default, one for each of the M4's four fast cores.

162
00:07:56,996 --> 00:08:00,945
That gives a two to two and a half times speed up over one thread.

163
00:08:00,945 --> 00:08:04,414
Adding the six slower efficiency cores gives nothing more.

164
00:08:05,375 --> 00:08:08,754
The same code runs on the GPU by changing one setting.

165
00:08:08,754 --> 00:08:14,697
At eight thousand tokens, dense takes two hundred and ninety six milliseconds, ours twenty two:

166
00:08:14,697 --> 00:08:16,074
thirteen times faster.

167
00:08:16,074 --> 00:08:21,517
At sixteen thousand tokens, PyTorch's built in attention runs out of memory on the GPU,

168
00:08:21,517 --> 00:08:24,520
while ours finishes in thirty four milliseconds.

169
00:08:24,875 --> 00:08:26,114
One limit we found:

170
00:08:26,114 --> 00:08:30,025
on the GPU, every small operation has a fixed start up cost.

171
00:08:30,025 --> 00:08:35,763
We launch a few small operations per query block, so below about twelve percent density,

172
00:08:35,763 --> 00:08:39,088
the time stops falling, at around ten milliseconds.

173
00:08:40,042 --> 00:08:42,645
So why don't we reach the full ideal speed up?

174
00:08:42,645 --> 00:08:43,381
Four reasons.

175
00:08:43,381 --> 00:08:46,777
Each block has a fixed bookkeeping cost that doesn't shrink.

176
00:08:46,777 --> 00:08:48,135
Copying data costs time.

177
00:08:48,135 --> 00:08:51,418
Many small operations are less efficient than one big one,

178
00:08:51,418 --> 00:08:55,380
which is also why sparse is slower than dense when nothing is skipped.

179
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:58,776
And uneven rows waste work if you force them into one shape.

180
00:08:58,776 --> 00:09:02,115
These are the computer architecture lessons of the project.

181
00:09:03,083 --> 00:09:03,652
To sum up.

182
00:09:03,652 --> 00:09:07,631
We built a block sparse attention kernel and a benchmarking framework,

183
00:09:07,631 --> 00:09:10,132
and measured them on a laptop's CPU and GPU.

184
00:09:10,132 --> 00:09:14,281
With a sliding window, attention grows linearly instead of quadratically:

185
00:09:14,281 --> 00:09:18,261
fourteen point seven times faster than dense at eight thousand tokens,

186
00:09:18,261 --> 00:09:21,273
with about seven hundred and sixty times less memory.

187
00:09:21,273 --> 00:09:24,854
The saving only appears when skipped blocks are never computed.

188
00:09:24,854 --> 00:09:29,345
And how you organise the remaining work decides how close you get to the ideal.

189
00:09:29,708 --> 00:09:30,755
Thanks for watching.
